prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a 3D tank battle game. The player controls a tank on the battlefield and must destroy 5 enemy tanks.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import through ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Battlefield Terrain]
(1) A flat battlefield measuring 100×100 units, with the ground using a light green/sand-colored material (MeshStandardMaterial);
(2) 10 randomly placed obstacle blocks (BoxGeometry, 2-3 units high, with random width and depth of 3-5 units) serving as cover;
(3) Low walls surround the perimeter of the battlefield to prevent tanks from leaving the boundaries;
(4) Use a light blue background color for the sky;

[Player Tank Model]
(5) Build the tank from geometries combined in a Group:
  - Hull: BoxGeometry (length 4 × height 1.2 × width 3), dark green;
  - Turret: CylinderGeometry (radius 1.2, height 0.8), mounted at the center of the top of the hull, a slightly brighter dark green;
  - Barrel: CylinderGeometry (radius 0.15, length 3), extending from the front of the turret;
(6) The tank casts shadows;

[Player Controls]
(7) W/S keys: move forward/backward, with a hull movement speed of 6 units/s;
(8) A/D keys: turn the hull left/right (rotate the hull's yaw), with a turning speed of 1.5 rad/s;
(9) Horizontal mouse movement: independently rotate the turret (turretAngle, relative to the hull);
(10) Left-click: fire a shell;

[Shell Physics]
(11) Use SphereGeometry (radius 0.2) for shells, spawning them at the muzzle of the barrel;
(12) Shells have an initial speed of 30 units/s and are fired along the barrel's direction;
(13) Shells are affected by gravity and follow a parabolic trajectory, with g = 9.8 m/s²;
(14) A shell disappears when it hits the ground or an obstacle, producing an explosion particle effect (10-20 small cubes spreading outward and then disappearing);
(15) A shell that hits an enemy tank deals 50 damage;

[Enemy Tank AI]
(16) 5 enemy tanks, using the same model as the player but with red-toned materials;
(17) Enemy HP = 150, with an HP bar above each tank's head (billboard);
(18) AI behavior: randomly patrol and move at 3 units/s; fire toward the player once every 3-5 seconds;
(19) An enemy shell that hits the player deals 25 damage;
(20) When an enemy tank dies (HP<=0): turn it gray, stop its movement and firing, and emit black smoke (particle effect optional);

[Player Status]
(21) Player HP = 200;

[Camera]
(22) Third-person camera: follow the player's tank from behind and above the tank (offset approximately (0, 8, -12)), looking toward the area in front of the tank;

[HUD Display]
(23) Display the following in the interface:
  - Player health (id="playerHP")
  - Number of shells fired (id="shellsFired")
  - Number of surviving enemy tanks (id="enemyCount")
  - Score (id="score", +100 points for each tank destroyed)

[State Exposure]
(24) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame, containing:
  - playerHP: number (player health)
  - shellsFired: number (total number of shells fired)
  - enemyCount: number (number of surviving enemy tanks)
  - score: number (score)
  - playerPos: { x, y, z } (player tank position)
  - turretAngle: number (turret angle relative to the hull, in radians)
  - enemies: [{ x, y, z, hp, alive }] (state array for the 5 enemy tanks)
  - activeShells: number (number of shells currently in flight)

Output a single HTML file that can run by being opened directly in a browser.
